From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 11:37:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(log): a log over the limit keeps its last lines

clean_log.sh emptied homeproxy.log, sing-box-c.log and sing-box-s.log
once they grew over 50 KB. A log grows like that when something goes
wrong (a burst of errors, or the info level while looking into a
problem), and within three minutes those lines were gone together with
the rest: the log page showed an empty log just when it was needed.

Now such a log keeps its last half (25 KB, whole lines). sing-box opens
its logs with O_APPEND and the init script appends with >>, so the file
is rewritten in place and writers go on at its end. If the copy fails,
the log is emptied as before.

Checked with busybox sh: a 58 KB log is cut to 25 KB of whole lines
ending with the newest one, the inode is kept, a writer holding the file
open with O_APPEND goes on at the new end with no NUL bytes, a small log
is left alone and no temporary file remains.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/root/etc/homeproxy/scripts/clean_log.sh b/root/etc/homeproxy/scripts/clean_log.sh
index c0d8f10..f0cae46 100755
--- a/root/etc/homeproxy/scripts/clean_log.sh
+++ b/root/etc/homeproxy/scripts/clean_log.sh
@@ -6,6 +6,10 @@
 NAME="homeproxy"
 
 log_max_size="50" #KB
+# A log over the limit keeps its last half (whole lines) instead of being
+# emptied: the lines that made it grow are usually the ones that matter.
+# sing-box opens its logs with O_APPEND, so rewriting a file in place is safe.
+log_keep_size="$((log_max_size * 1024 / 2))" #bytes
 main_log_file="/var/run/$NAME/$NAME.log"
 singc_log_file="/var/run/$NAME/sing-box-c.log"
 sings_log_file="/var/run/$NAME/sing-box-s.log"
@@ -14,6 +18,12 @@ while true; do
 	sleep 180
 	for i in "$main_log_file" "$singc_log_file" "$sings_log_file"; do
 		[ -s "$i" ] || continue
-		[ "$(( $(ls -l "$i" | awk -F ' ' '{print $5}') / 1024 >= log_max_size))" -eq "0" ] || echo "" > "$i"
+		[ "$(( $(ls -l "$i" | awk -F ' ' '{print $5}') / 1024 >= log_max_size))" -eq "0" ] && continue
+		if tail -c "$log_keep_size" "$i" | sed '1d' > "$i.tmp"; then
+			cat "$i.tmp" > "$i"
+		else
+			echo "" > "$i"
+		fi
+		rm -f "$i.tmp"
 	done
 done
